    "https://www.lec.edu/undergraduate-degrees": {
      "title": "Undergraduate Degrees",
      "description": "Undergraduate Degrees | Lake Erie College top of page Skip to Main Content Search Apply Give VISIT Make a Payment LEO More Use tab to navigate through the menu items. Menu Undergraduate Degrees The Power to Write Your Story You are the author of your story. Similar to novelists, you may have the inspiration for a great story, know your plot, and have clarity on the protagonist’s journey, but do you know how you are going to move that storyline from point A to point B? From freshman convocation to graduation day, we help you write your own story. We value the experiences that help you identify and nurture your strengths. Within the schools of Lake Erie College, you will identify your passions and develop the steps needed to take you to the epilogue. ​ Tailored Education Experience​ Lake Erie College offers a number of majors and minors to customize your education. Our faculty and staff will assist in defining the steps needed to get you to your goals. Let us help you create a well-rounded and academically rigorous experience. We have an established sense of community at Lake Erie College. This is shown through collaborating on campus wide sustainability efforts, partnering with local high schools to support educational access and more. Whatever your idea of success, LEC has the combination of majors, minors, and extra-curricular opportunities to get you there. Explore our schools, our majors, featured courses, and meet our faculty! ​ 4+1 Programs Undergraduate students at Lake Erie College who meet eligibility requirements may enroll in graduate-level courses that count as electives toward their bachelor’s degree while also fulfilling requirements for a graduate program. This pathway allows students to earn both an undergraduate and a graduate degree in just five years. ​ Select School helpful links to Tailor your education experience Academic Calendar Tower Honors Program Study Abroad Partnerships and Accelerated Programs Office of Student Life School of Business School of Education School of Science, Animal Studies, and Human Services Arts & Humanities School of Business Accounting Business Administration Criminal Justice Cybersecurity Entrepreneurship Finance Human Resource Management Marketing Psychology Sport Management Sport Studies ​ Broad Curriculum Options Choose from general studies in Management or specialize in: Finance Accounting Human Resource Management Sport Management Sport Studies Marketing ​ Our unique blend of business and professional studies education and liberal arts prepares you to adapt, thrive, and excel in today’s fast-changing professional landscape. ​ Specialized Programs Gain real-world experience through hands-on learning opportunities: LEC Tax Clinic Assist community members with tax preparation while building practical financial skills. Market Research Group Work with businesses to analyze data, address key research questions, and deliver actionable insights. ​ Engaged Faculty Our faculty brings extensive real-world experience into the classroom, enriching your education with practical knowledge. Dedicated to your success, they provide personalized guidance to help you reach your academic and professional goals. View Majors meet the dean of the school of business Amanda Curtis, PhD Dean of the School of Business; Associate Professor of Sport Management Phone 1 (440)-375-7167 Mail acurtis@lec.edu Location College Hall, 301 About Dr. Curtis Dr. Amanda Curtis earned her Ph.D. in Health and Sport Studies from the University of Iowa in 2013. She also holds an M.S. Ed in Sports Science & Wellness Education from the University of Akron and a B.A. from Albion College in Physical Education and English. Dr. Curtis’s primary areas of interest involve issues of gender and physicality within the fields of sport history and sport sociology as well as representations of female athletes and physicality in the media. She also presents original research in these areas at various national and international academic conferences. She teaches courses within the Sport Studies curriculum such as: Gender, Sexuality and Sport; History of Sport in America; Sports in Education; Sport Sociology; Sport, Nationalism and Culture; and Sport Communication, among others. Dr. Curtis was recognized for her efforts in the classroom with the Ohio Magazine Excellence in Education award as well as the LEC Tibby for Faculty member of the year. ​ Education and Teaching Majors Adolescent–Young Adult Education Education: Multi-Age Education: Primary (PK–5) Intervention Specialist – Mild to Moderate Needs ​ Program Overview Our Education programs prepare future teachers, coaches, and specialists to make a meaningful impact in today’s classrooms. Through a blend of academic coursework, field experiences, and mentorship, you will develop the skills needed to support diverse learners and thrive as an educator. ​ Special Accreditation Lake Erie College has been awarded specialized national accreditation for its education programs with the Council for the Accreditation of Educator Preparation (CAEP). CAEP is the recognized accrediting body for educator preparation programs, ensuring high-quality education for future teachers. CAEP's rigorous standards focus on continuous improvement, promoting research-based teaching methods, and producing highly effective educators who are prepared to meet the needs of students. Accreditation by CAEP signifies a program’s commitment to excellence and accountability, ensuring that graduates are ready to succeed in the classroom and improve student outcomes. Download the CAEP Accreditation Measures ​ Accreditation status: Accreditation status: Full Accreditation These programs were part of the 2023 review: AYA Chemistry Initial-Licensure Level Baccalaureate AYA English/Language Arts Education Initial-Licensure Level Baccalaureate AYA Life Science (Biology) Initial-Licensure Level Baccalaureate AYA Mathematics Education Initial-Licensure Level Baccalaureate AYA Social Studies Education Initial-Licensure Level Baccalaureate Computer Science Endorsement UG Initial-Licensure Level Endorsement Only Elementary Education Prek-5th Initial-Licensure Level Baccalaureate Multi-Age Spanish Initial-Licensure Level Baccalaureate Multi-Age Visual Arts Initial-Licensure Level Baccalaureate SPED: Intervention Specialist Initial-Licensure Level Baccalaureate SPED: Intervention Specialist online Initial-Licensure Level Master's TESOL UG Initial-Licensure Level Endorsement Only ​ View Majors meet the dean of the school of education Greg Rothwell, PhD Dean of the School of Education, Assistant Professor of Education Title IX Investigator Phone 1 (440) 375-7371 Mail grothwell@lec.edu Location Garfield Center About Dr. Rothwell ​ Greg Rothwell is currently the Program Director of Education & Professional Studies. Previously, he has served as assistant and head principal of Follansbee Middle School and Brooke High School, in Brooke County, West Virginia. Before his career in administrative positions, Greg taught history at Brooke High School. During his tenure at Brooke High School, Greg served on numerous committees including groups focusing on curriculum and technology, establishing many Advanced Placement courses, and academic-based competition teams. Also, Greg has served in many leadership roles, including the Brooke High School faculty senate chair, social studies department chair, member of the school leadership team, local school improvement council, and a regional advisory committee for professional development. As a leader, Greg has established an emphasis on STEM education at the district level, working with curriculum directors and faculty. After receiving grant money, Greg and his team traveled throughout the country to learn about STEM-related educational techniques and technologies. Greg earned his Ph.D. from Robert Morris University in Moon Township, Pennsylvania, studying Instructional Management and Leadership. His dissertation title is \"An Updated Look at Adopters and Non-Adopters of Educational Technology Among Teachers in a Rural Setting\". He has also earned an M.A in Teaching and Learning with Technology from Ashford University (IA), an M.A in Educational Leadership from Wheeling Jesuit University (WV), and a B.A. in Secondary Education with an emphasis on social studies from West Liberty University (WV). ​ Science, Animal Studies, and Human SERVICES Majors Biology Chemistry Environmental Science Equine Business Administration Equine Facility Management Equine Pre-Veterinary Science Equine Teacher/Trainer Equine Therapeutic Horsemanship Exercise Science Mathematics Nutrition ​ Broad Curriculum Options Science at Lake Erie College offers diverse academic pathways in the sciences, mathematics, and equine studies. Students may pursue a traditional science curriculum or specialize in one of our equine-focused or health-related programs. Our programs blend scientific inquiry, hands-on research, industry experience, and personalized faculty mentorship—equipping graduates to excel in careers ranging from laboratory science to veterinary medicine, equine management, and human performance. ​ Specialized Programs & Unique Opportunities Gain real-world experience through immersive, hands-on learning across scientific, mathematical, and equine fields: Equine Studies at the George M. Humphrey Equestrian Center Students in equine programs train at our nationally recognized equestrian facility, gaining professional-level experience in horse care, facility operations, and equine training. Practical learning is integrated into daily coursework, preparing students for careers in management, instruction, therapy, and veterinary support. Therapeutic Horsemanship Students have the opportunity to work directly in a PATH, International Premier Center under certified instructors to develop skills in adaptive instruction, rider support, and equine-assisted services.  Graduates are prepared for certification or administration in a rapidly growing area of the equine industry. Students also earn community service and invaluable experience as volunteers in our in-house therapeutic program. Undergraduate Research & Laboratory Experience Science students engage in faculty-guided research, laboratory work, and field studies that build technical and analytical expertise. Pre-Veterinary Pathway Equine Pre-Veterinary Science majors follow a professionally aligned curriculum designed to prepare them for veterinary school admission, with opportunities for hands-on animal care, internships, and faculty mentorship. ​ Engaged Faculty Our Science faculty includes scientists, mathematicians, equine professionals, and industry practitioners who bring real-world experience directly into the classroom, lab, and barn. Dedicated to your success, they provide personalized guidance, individualized instruction, and professional mentorship—helping you become a confident, capable leader in your chosen field. ​ Careers for Science Students Lake Erie College Science graduates are prepared for a wide range of careers, including: Veterinary medicine Equine facility management Therapeutic horsemanship instruction Laboratory research Environmental consulting Data science and analytics Exercise physiology Health and nutrition fields Science education Graduate study in STEMM and health professions ​ View Majors meet the Leadership of the school of Science, Animal Studies, and Human Services Laura Stockhaus, MBA Dean of Equine Studies; Assistant Professor of Equine Studies Phone 1 (440)-375-8051 Mail lstockhaus@lec.edu Location Equestrian Center, 214 About Laura Stockhaus ​ ​ Laura Stockhaus has been a member of the Lake Erie College faculty since 2013. She holds a Bachelor of Science in Equine Facility Management from Otterbein University and an MBA from Lake Erie College. Her work reflects a strong commitment to experiential learning and the integration of academic instruction with hands-on equine industry practice. Professor Stockhaus brings extensive professional experience in equine facility management, horse training, and riding instruction across a variety of organizations in Northeast Ohio. She previously served as the Equestrian Director at Andrews Osborne Academy, where she managed facility operations, coached two Interscholastic Equestrian Association (IEA) teams, organized nationally rated horse shows, and developed and implemented equine studies curricula at the middle and high school levels. She also served as Equestrian Director of Red Oak Camp, a long-standing nonprofit organization that played an influential role in shaping her approach to equine education. At Lake Erie College, Professor Stockhaus teaches Equine Business and Equine Nutrition, as well as select riding and training courses. She also oversees the College’s public horse shows and equestrian events, providing students with meaningful, real-world learning opportunities that support professional development within the equine industry. Arts and Humanities Majors English Fine Arts History Italian Studies ​ Program Overview The Arts and Humanities programs at Lake Erie College offer a rich liberal arts education rooted in creativity, critical thinking, cultural understanding, and effective communication. Students explore human expression and global perspectives while building versatile skills that prepare them for a wide range of professional and academic pathways. ​ Specialized Opportunities Students in General Studies gain hands-on experience through immersive coursework, creative projects, and experiential learning opportunities: English Study British and American literature while sharpening your writing, research, and analytical skills—foundations that prepare you for graduate school and careers in writing, communication, education, and more. Fine Arts Engage in studio work and performance across Dance, Music, Theatre, Visual Art, or a Multi-Disciplinary track. Learn from artists and practitioners while developing technical skill and creative expression. History Explore American and world history through research-driven coursework, archival projects, and seminar discussions that build strong analytical and communication skills. Italian Studies Immerse yourself in Italy’s language, culture, and artistic heritage through coursework, internships, and study abroad experiences that develop global awareness and cultural fluency. ​ Engaged Faculty Arts and Humanities faculty are passionate scholars, artists, and educators who bring deep expertise and personal mentorship to every classroom. Through close collaboration on projects and individualized guidance, they help you refine your academic interests, strengthen your creative voice, and prepare for meaningful careers and advanced study. ​ Careers for Arts and Humanities Students Graduates from Arts and Humanities disciplines pursue careers in: Education Arts and performance Museums and cultural institutions Writing, editing, and publishing Public service and government Research and archival work Communications and media Graduate and professional programs Lake Erie College graduates enter the world with strong cultural literacy, communication excellence, and the analytical skills that employers value across industries. View Majors meet the dean of Academic and Student Engagement Jenn Miller, PhD Dean of Academic and Student Engagement; Title IX Appeals Coordinator Phone 1 (440) 375-7379 Mail jemiller@lec.edu Location James F. Lincoln Learning Commons About Dr. Miller ​ ​ Dr. Jenn Miller is the Executive Director of the Learning Commons and has more than 20 years of experience in education. Currently completing her Doctorate in Education (EdD) in Educational Technology at Walden University, she specializes in instructional design, digital literacy, and online learning. Dr. Miller holds a Master of Education in Literacy and Library Media Science and a Bachelor’s in Political Science from Quincy University. Her expertise spans academic support, library media, and the integration of technology in education. She is passionate about enhancing learning experiences through digital tools, fostering communication, and promoting effective online learning strategies. ​ Undergrad List Visit Campus Overview Apply Request Info 391 W Washington St, Painesville, Ohio, 44077 440.375.7000 © 2026 Lake Erie College Campus Map Catalog and School Handbook Compliments and Complaints Consumer Information Make a Payment APPLY VISIT Give Lake Erie Online Close About History News and Announcements Faculty & Staff Board of Directors Accreditation Events Admissions Campus Tours Tuition & Fees Housing & Dining Financial Aid College Credit Plus (CCP) Office of Veterans and Military Service New Student Orientation Academics Academic Calendar Undergraduate Degrees Graduate Degrees Physician Assistant Program (PA) Study Abroad Partnerships & Pre-Professional Programs Professional Teacher Pathway Registrar Student Life Clubs & Organizations Tower Honors Program Career Development Career Closet Lincoln Learning Commons Campus Safety Non-Degree Programs Professional Development for Teachers Equine Studies Athletics Arts & Culture Alumni & Friends Parents & Family Careers Facilities Bookstore Campus Gear LAKE ERIE ONLINE bottom of page Double click the content to see meaning"
    },
    "https://www.lec.edu/graduate-degrees": {
      "title": "Graduate Degrees",
      "description": "Graduate Degrees | Lake Erie College top of page Skip to Main Content Search Apply Give VISIT Make a Payment LEO More Use tab to navigate through the menu items. Menu graduate Degrees Our Mission for Graduate Students At Lake Erie College, we provide highly personalized education grounded in the liberal arts tradition. Guided by hope and care, we empower our students to lead lives of personal significance and professional success as engaged global citizens. Through a focus on applicable skills—such as communication, critical inquiry, and problem-solving—our Graduate Studies faculty guide students toward the highest levels of personal and professional fulfillment. 4+1 Programs Undergraduate students at Lake Erie College who meet eligibility requirements may enroll in graduate-level courses that count as electives toward their bachelor’s degree while also fulfilling requirements for a graduate program. This pathway allows students to earn both an undergraduate and a graduate degree in just five years. ​ ​ APPLY helpful links to Tailor your education experience Apply Academic Calendar Tower Honors Program Study Abroad Partnerships and Accelerated Programs Office of Student Life Physician Assistant Program Master of Business Administration Master of Ed. Leadership Master of Education Master of Ed. - Non-Licensure Master of Professional Studies Lead the Future of Business The Master of Business Administration (MBA) at Lake Erie College is designed to develop strong leadership and strategic management skills for today’s dynamic business environment. Offered fully online, the program equips students with practical knowledge and advanced decision-making tools to excel in managerial and executive roles across industries. ​ Concentrations in business administration Students may choose from the following MBA concentrations to customize their studies: General Management – Build a broad foundation in business leadership and organizational strategy. Healthcare Administration – Focus on the unique business and leadership needs within hospitals, pharmaceutical companies, and medical technology organizations. ​ What You Can Do with the Degree Graduates of the MBA program are prepared to take on leadership and management roles in a wide variety of fields. Career paths include: Business and Operations Management Healthcare Administration Finance and Banking Consulting Marketing and Communications Nonprofit and Government Leadership Entrepreneurship ​ APPLY View Curriculum for MBA Contact MBA Admissions Leading Change in Education The Master of Educational Leadership (M.Ed.) – Non-Licensure at Lake Erie College prepares educators and professionals to become effective leaders in schools and educational organizations without pursuing principal licensure. This 36-credit, fully online program is designed for individuals who want to expand their leadership skills, guide instructional improvement, and influence positive change within their schools or districts. Through coursework in transformational leadership, instructional leadership, data-driven decision making, school law, culturally responsive leadership, and school finance, students develop the knowledge and practical leadership skills needed to support teachers, improve student outcomes, and lead meaningful school initiatives. The program provides flexibility for working professionals and includes the option to complete a thesis or capstone project, allowing students to investigate and address real-world challenges in education. This program is ideal for educators who want to take on leadership roles such as department chair, instructional leader, or program coordinator while remaining in the classroom or working in educational organizations. ​ Core Leadership Coursework Students complete 36 semester hours of graduate coursework, including: Transformational Leadership Instructional Leadership School Law and Ethics Safe and Supportive Schools Community Relations and Collaboration Culturally Responsive Leadership Leadership in Special Education Data-Driven Decision Making Innovative Leadership and Teaching School Finance Students may choose to complete a thesis or capstone project. What You Can Do with the Degree Graduates of the M.Ed. in Educational Leadership (Non-Licensure) are prepared for leadership roles across educational settings, including: Instructional Leader or Teacher Leader Department Chair or Grade-Level Leader Curriculum or Instructional Coordinator Professional Development Leader Program Director or Education Administrator Educational Consultant or Instructional Coach ​ APPLY View Curriculum for M.Ed. View Curriculum for MEL meet the dean of the school of education Greg Rothwell, PhD Dean of the School of Education, Assistant Professor of Education Title IX Investigator Phone 1 (440) 375-7371 Mail grothwell@lec.edu Location Garfield Center About Dr. Rothwell ​ Greg Rothwell is currently the Program Director of Education & Professional Studies. Previously, he has served as assistant and head principal of Follansbee Middle School and Brooke High School, in Brooke County, West Virginia. Before his career in administrative positions, Greg taught history at Brooke High School. During his tenure at Brooke High School, Greg served on numerous committees including groups focusing on curriculum and technology, establishing many Advanced Placement courses, and academic-based competition teams. Also, Greg has served in many leadership roles, including the Brooke High School faculty senate chair, social studies department chair, member of the school leadership team, local school improvement council, and a regional advisory committee for professional development. As a leader, Greg has established an emphasis on STEM education at the district level, working with curriculum directors and faculty. After receiving grant money, Greg and his team traveled throughout the country to learn about STEM-related educational techniques and technologies. Greg earned his Ph.D. from Robert Morris University in Moon Township, Pennsylvania, studying Instructional Management and Leadership. His dissertation title is \"An Updated Look at Adopters and Non-Adopters of Educational Technology Among Teachers in a Rural Setting\". He has also earned an M.A in Teaching and Learning with Technology from Ashford University (IA), an M.A in Educational Leadership from Wheeling Jesuit University (WV), and a B.A. in Secondary Education with an emphasis on social studies from West Liberty University (WV). ​ Advancing Your Professional Edge The Master of Professional Studies (MPS) degree is designed for working professionals seeking career advancement or transition without pausing their employment. Offered fully online, the program emphasizes real-world skills such as critical thinking, communication, collaboration, strategy, and ethics—applicable across multiple industries. ​ ​ What You Can Do with the Degree The MPS program prepares graduates to thrive in leadership, administrative, and analytical roles across a range of fields, including: Public Relations and Communications Sports Management Real Estate and Property Development Technology and Data Management Human Resources Public Administration Corporate and Nonprofit Leadership Entrepreneurial and Managerial Roles ​ APPLY View Curriculum for MPS Contact MPS Admissions Proven, Award-Winning Program Lake Erie College offers one of Ohio’s top Physician Assistant programs. Exceeds average PANCE pass rate since 2016 and 98.7% first-time pass rate—all 152 graduates are certified PAs. 100% employment rate since 2016, exceeding the national average. Nationally recognized, including six-time quarterfinalist and a top-five national finish in the AAPA Medical Challenge Bowl. ​ Exceptional Clinical Experiences Students train with University Hospitals, Cleveland Clinic, Lake Health, Akron Children’s Hospital, the VA Medical Center, and other leading healthcare organizations. Northeast Ohio offers abundant, high-quality career opportunities in diverse medical settings. ​ Integrated, Comprehensive Curriculum ​ Module-based curriculum with problem-based learning. Three semesters of pharmacology aligned with clinical modules. Tuition includes textbooks, anatomy software, board review resources, medical equipment, scrubs, and lab coats. Access to ultrasound training, Anatomage virtual dissection, rhythm-training software, and high-fidelity manikins (including the NextGen Harvey® simulator). Early clinical exposure begins within the first weeks of training. ​ Outstanding Faculty Small class sizes and lab groups of 6–8 students ensure personalized, hands-on instruction. Medical Director serves as the UH Eastern Regional Chief Medical Officer. Faculty actively practice medicine and bring 100+ years of clinical experience and 55+ years of PA education experience. Anatomy & Physiology taught by dual MD/PhD faculty. Faculty have secured multiple grants through national PA and health organizations. ​ More Information for M.S.P.A.S. Shaping Tomorrow’s Classrooms The Master of Education (M.Ed.) at Lake Erie College helps educators deepen their teaching expertise and expand their impact in the classroom. Offered 100% online, the program emphasizes practical application, professional growth, and the creation of equitable, inclusive learning environments that support the success of all learners. Through focused coursework and specialized concentrations, educators develop advanced instructional strategies, strengthen their content expertise, and gain the skills needed to support diverse students across a variety of educational settings. ​ Concentrations in Education Students can personalize their program by choosing from a variety of concentrations, including: Athletic Coaching Reading Science with Reading Endorsement Mild to Moderate Intervention Specialist (license) Computer Science Endorsement Teaching English to Speakers of Other Languages (T-ESOL endorsement) ​ What You Can Do with the Degree Graduates of the M.Ed. program are prepared to expand their expertise and advance their careers in specialized areas of education, including: Athletic Coach Reading or Literacy Specialist Special Education Teacher or Intervention Specialist Computer Science or Technology Teacher English Learner (EL) / TESOL Instructor ​ APPLY View Curriculum for M.Ed. View Curriculum for MEL meet the dean of the school of education Greg Rothwell, PhD Dean of the School of Education, Assistant Professor of Education Title IX Investigator Phone 1 (440) 375-7371 Mail grothwell@lec.edu Location Garfield Center About Dr. Rothwell ​ Greg Rothwell is currently the Program Director of Education & Professional Studies. Previously, he has served as assistant and head principal of Follansbee Middle School and Brooke High School, in Brooke County, West Virginia. Before his career in administrative positions, Greg taught history at Brooke High School. During his tenure at Brooke High School, Greg served on numerous committees including groups focusing on curriculum and technology, establishing many Advanced Placement courses, and academic-based competition teams. Also, Greg has served in many leadership roles, including the Brooke High School faculty senate chair, social studies department chair, member of the school leadership team, local school improvement council, and a regional advisory committee for professional development. As a leader, Greg has established an emphasis on STEM education at the district level, working with curriculum directors and faculty. After receiving grant money, Greg and his team traveled throughout the country to learn about STEM-related educational techniques and technologies. Greg earned his Ph.D. from Robert Morris University in Moon Township, Pennsylvania, studying Instructional Management and Leadership. His dissertation title is \"An Updated Look at Adopters and Non-Adopters of Educational Technology Among Teachers in a Rural Setting\". He has also earned an M.A in Teaching and Learning with Technology from Ashford University (IA), an M.A in Educational Leadership from Wheeling Jesuit University (WV), and a B.A. in Secondary Education with an emphasis on social studies from West Liberty University (WV). ​ Leading Change in Education The Master of Educational Leadership (M.Ed.) – Non-Licensure at Lake Erie College prepares educators and professionals to become effective leaders in schools and educational organizations without pursuing principal licensure. This 36-credit, fully online program is designed for individuals who want to expand their leadership skills, guide instructional improvement, and influence positive change within their schools or districts. Through coursework in transformational leadership, instructional leadership, data-driven decision making, school law, culturally responsive leadership, and school finance, students develop the knowledge and practical leadership skills needed to support teachers, improve student outcomes, and lead meaningful school initiatives. The program provides flexibility for working professionals and includes the option to complete a thesis or capstone project, allowing students to investigate and address real-world challenges in education. This program is ideal for educators who want to take on leadership roles such as department chair, instructional leader, or program coordinator while remaining in the classroom or working in educational organizations. ​ Core Leadership Coursework Students complete 36 semester hours of graduate coursework, including: Transformational Leadership Instructional Leadership School Law and Ethics Safe and Supportive Schools Community Relations and Collaboration Culturally Responsive Leadership Leadership in Special Education Data-Driven Decision Making Innovative Leadership and Teaching School Finance Students may choose to complete a thesis or capstone project. What You Can Do with the Degree Graduates of the M.Ed. in Educational Leadership (Non-Licensure) are prepared for leadership roles across educational settings, including: Instructional Leader or Teacher Leader Department Chair or Grade-Level Leader Curriculum or Instructional Coordinator Professional Development Leader Program Director or Education Administrator Educational Consultant or Instructional Coach ​ APPLY View Curriculum for M.Ed. View Curriculum for MEL meet the dean of the school of education Greg Rothwell, PhD Dean of the School of Education, Assistant Professor of Education Title IX Investigator Phone 1 (440) 375-7371 Mail grothwell@lec.edu Location Garfield Center About Dr. Rothwell ​ Greg Rothwell is currently the Program Director of Education & Professional Studies. Previously, he has served as assistant and head principal of Follansbee Middle School and Brooke High School, in Brooke County, West Virginia. Before his career in administrative positions, Greg taught history at Brooke High School. During his tenure at Brooke High School, Greg served on numerous committees including groups focusing on curriculum and technology, establishing many Advanced Placement courses, and academic-based competition teams. Also, Greg has served in many leadership roles, including the Brooke High School faculty senate chair, social studies department chair, member of the school leadership team, local school improvement council, and a regional advisory committee for professional development. As a leader, Greg has established an emphasis on STEM education at the district level, working with curriculum directors and faculty. After receiving grant money, Greg and his team traveled throughout the country to learn about STEM-related educational techniques and technologies. Greg earned his Ph.D. from Robert Morris University in Moon Township, Pennsylvania, studying Instructional Management and Leadership. His dissertation title is \"An Updated Look at Adopters and Non-Adopters of Educational Technology Among Teachers in a Rural Setting\". He has also earned an M.A in Teaching and Learning with Technology from Ashford University (IA), an M.A in Educational Leadership from Wheeling Jesuit University (WV), and a B.A. in Secondary Education with an emphasis on social studies from West Liberty University (WV). ​ Office of Financial Aid Visit Campus Overview Apply Request Info 391 W Washington St, Painesville, Ohio, 44077 440.375.7000 © 2026 Lake Erie College Campus Map Catalog and School Handbook Compliments and Complaints Consumer Information Make a Payment APPLY VISIT Give Lake Erie Online Close About History News and Announcements Faculty & Staff Board of Directors Accreditation Events Admissions Campus Tours Tuition & Fees Housing & Dining Financial Aid College Credit Plus (CCP) Office of Veterans and Military Service New Student Orientation Academics Academic Calendar Undergraduate Degrees Graduate Degrees Physician Assistant Program (PA) Study Abroad Partnerships & Pre-Professional Programs Professional Teacher Pathway Registrar Student Life Clubs & Organizations Tower Honors Program Career Development Career Closet Lincoln Learning Commons Campus Safety Non-Degree Programs Professional Development for Teachers Equine Studies Athletics Arts & Culture Alumni & Friends Parents & Family Careers Facilities Bookstore Campus Gear LAKE ERIE ONLINE bottom of page Double click the content to see meaning"
