    "https://www.lec.edu/safety-and-security": {
      "title": "Campus Safety",
      "description": "Campus Safety | Lake Erie College top of page Skip to Main Content Search Apply Give VISIT Make a Payment LEO More Use tab to navigate through the menu items. Menu campus safety & Security Our Team and Mission The Department of Safety and Security, located in Holden Center, Room 110, provides a wide range of services designed to support the safety and well-being of the Lake Erie College community. ​ The men and women of the Safety and Security Department come from diverse backgrounds, including military and law enforcement service. Officers work closely with the City of Painesville Police Department and other Lake County agencies to ensure a safe campus environment. The department conducts 24-hour, seven-days-a-week foot and vehicle patrols and is charged by the College administration with: Enforcing College policies and regulations Cooperating with state and municipal authorities Monitoring campus facilities for fire, safety, and health hazards When special events are held on campus or circumstances require additional coverage, security support is enhanced through the use of off-duty City of Painesville Police officers. ​ ​ ​ Contact Information Department of Safety and Security Holden Center, Room 110 (440) 375-7575 Officers are available 24 hours a day, 7 days a week. ​ ​ ​ Rick Kline Director of Safety and Security Phone 1 (440)- 375-7512 Mail rkline@lec.edu Location Holden Center, 110 SCHOOLS List Statistics & Policies Safety Services Security Tips The Jeanne Clery Act The Jeanne Clery Act is a federal consumer protection law enacted in 1990 that requires colleges and universities receiving federal funding to disclose information about campus crime and safety efforts. Lake Erie College fulfills this requirement through the publication of its Annual Security Report, which provides information about crime statistics and campus safety policies. ​ Campus Safety Resources Crime Statistics Emergency Response Plan Campus Safety & Security Policies Fire Safety Report ​ Lake Erie College discloses all reported hazing violations. There have been no violations of the College’s Anti-Hazing Policy in the past five years. ​ Lost and Found & Escort Services The Department of Safety and Security maintains a Lost and Found and offers a walking security escort service to all members of the College community. Escorts are available for travel between campus buildings or to parking areas, particularly during non-business hours. To request an escort, please call the Safety and Security office. ​ Security Tips While the Lake Erie College campus is generally safe, community members are encouraged to follow these safety guidelines: ​ Avoid walking alone at night; use the campus escort service or travel in groups. Keep residence hall rooms and offices locked when unattended. If leaving your room, lock the door and take your keys and personal belongings with you. Lock your vehicle and check inside and underneath before entering. Keep all residence hall doors, including fire escape doors, closed at all times—do not prop doors open. Do not admit individuals you do not know into residence halls. Guests should contact the resident they are visiting and wait to be escorted inside. Stay aware of your surroundings and report suspicious activity. Learn the locations of campus phones and emergency resources. Call (440) 375-7575 if something appears unusual or concerning. Trust your instincts. If a situation makes you uncomfortable, contact Security. Your safety comes first. ​ Office of Financial Aid Visit Campus Overview Apply Request Info 391 W Washington St, Painesville, Ohio, 44077 440.375.7000 © 2026 Lake Erie College Campus Map Catalog and School Handbook Compliments and Complaints Consumer Information Make a Payment APPLY VISIT Give Lake Erie Online Close About History News and Announcements Faculty & Staff Board of Directors Accreditation Events Admissions Campus Tours Tuition & Fees Housing & Dining Financial Aid College Credit Plus (CCP) Office of Veterans and Military Service New Student Orientation Academics Academic Calendar Undergraduate Degrees Graduate Degrees Physician Assistant Program (PA) Study Abroad Partnerships & Pre-Professional Programs Professional Teacher Pathway Registrar Student Life Clubs & Organizations Tower Honors Program Career Development Career Closet Lincoln Learning Commons Campus Safety Non-Degree Programs Professional Development for Teachers Equine Studies Athletics Arts & Culture Alumni & Friends Parents & Family Careers Facilities Bookstore Campus Gear LAKE ERIE ONLINE bottom of page Double click the content to see meaning"
    },
    "https://www.lec.edu/professional-development-teachers": {
      "title": "Professional Development for Teachers",
      "description": "Professional Development for Teachers | Lake Erie College top of page Skip to Main Content Search Apply Give VISIT Make a Payment LEO More Use tab to navigate through the menu items. Menu Professional development for Teachers Advanced Skills for Teachers Teachers can expand their expertise through graduate-level Professional Development Programs offered by the Center for Professional Development. Courses—taught by experienced K–12 educators—support license renewal and classroom innovation, with flexible options. ​ helpful links to Tailor your education experience Academic Calendar Tower Honors Program Study Abroad Partnerships and Accelerated Programs Office of Student Life Professional Teacher Pathway Athletic Administration Certification Computer Science Endorsement MMIS Licensure Only Reading Endorsement T-ESOL Endorsement Preparing Leaders for Athletic Programs The Graduate Certificate in Athletic Administration at Lake Erie College prepares educators and coaches to lead successful, student-centered athletic programs. This 18-credit, fully online certificate provides focused preparation for professionals who want to advance into athletic leadership roles, including positions as athletic directors and athletic program administrators. The program emphasizes the leadership, organizational, and compliance responsibilities required to manage modern athletic departments. Students develop practical skills in program administration, event and facility management, legal compliance, equity in athletics, marketing and community relations, and the supervision of coaches and staff. Coursework is designed to provide immediately applicable knowledge for professionals working in school or collegiate athletics. ​ Courses in the Certificate Program Students complete 18 credit hours in the following courses: Foundations of Athletic Administration Sports Facilities and Event Management Legal Issues and Risk Management in Athletics Title IX and Equity in Athletics Public Relations and Marketing for Athletic Programs Human Resources and Supervision in Athletics ​ What You Can Do with the Certificate Graduates of the Athletic Administration Certificate are prepared for leadership and management roles in educational athletics, including: Athletic Director Assistant Athletic Director Athletic Program Coordinator Head or Assistant Coach Athletic Facilities or Event Manager Student Activities or Athletics Coordinator School or District Athletic Program Administrator ​ Apply Expanding Opportunities Through Computer Science The Computer Science Endorsement at Lake Erie College prepares licensed educators to teach computer science and computational thinking in today’s technology-driven classrooms. Designed for working teachers, this program builds the knowledge and skills needed to introduce students to programming, problem solving, and the systems that power modern technology. Through coursework in programming, algorithms, computing systems, and the social impact of technology, educators learn how to help students develop critical thinking, logical reasoning, and digital problem-solving skills. The program also includes hands-on field experience in a computer science classroom, allowing candidates to apply their knowledge in authentic teaching environments. The Computer Science Endorsement can be added to any standard Ohio teaching license, including Primary Education (PK–5), Middle Childhood (4–9), Intervention Specialist (K–12), Adolescence to Young Adult (7–12), and Multi-Age (PK–12). ​ Coursework in Computer Science Students complete coursework that develops both technical knowledge and instructional practice, including: Programming and Algorithms I Programming and Algorithms II Computing Systems, Networks, and the Internet Technology and Social Value Field Experience or Computer Science Practicum ​ What You Can Do with the Endorsement Educators who complete the Computer Science Endorsement are prepared to expand computer science opportunities for students, including roles such as: Computer Science Teacher Technology and Coding Instructor STEM or Technology Educator Computational Thinking Curriculum Developer School Technology Integration Specialist Educator Supporting Computer Science and STEM Initiatives ​ Apply Supporting Students with Diverse Learning Needs The Mild to Moderate Intervention Specialist (MMIS-K12) Non-Degree Licensure Program at Lake Erie College is a 100% online, 18-credit licensure program that prepares educators to support students with diverse learning needs in inclusive and specialized classroom settings. The program emphasizes evidence-based instructional strategies, assessment, and data-driven decision-making, curriculum adaptation, positive behavior supports, and collaboration with families and professionals. Through focused coursework and field-based experiences, candidates learn to design individualized instruction, monitor student progress, and create supportive learning environments that promote both academic and personal success. This program is designed for licensed educators who wish to add the Mild to Moderate Intervention Specialist (K–12) license in Ohio, expanding their ability to serve students with disabilities across a variety of educational settings. ​ Program Structure & Timeline The MMIS program can be completed in as little as one year, depending on course load and prior coursework. Students complete: 18 semester hours of graduate coursework, including: Introduction to Mild to Moderate Intervention Evidence-Based Strategies Assessment and Data-Based Interventions Curriculum Planning and Strategies for Mild to Moderate Disabilities Positive Behavior Supports and Intervention Collaboration with Families and Professionals Reading coursework (if not previously completed) Ohio requires 12 semester hours of coursework in reading instruction, including phonics and the science of reading, for MMIS licensure. Candidates who have already completed this requirement as part of a prior program do not need to repeat it For those who still need it, Lake Erie College offers these courses fully online Reading coursework may be taken before, during, or after the MMIS program ​ Students who need both MMIS and reading coursework can still complete requirements in about one year, though this may require taking multiple courses at once. Field Experience Students complete 50 hours of field experience, embedded across coursework throughout the program. Field hours are flexible and can be completed in a setting of your choice Many candidates complete hours within their current roles (e.g., teachers, paraprofessionals, tutors, substitutes) Others arrange placements through local schools, colleagues, or personal connections ​ Licensure & Testing Requirements To add the MMIS (K–12) license in Ohio, candidates must: Provide evidence of passing a pedagogy exam (e.g., Praxis II or OAE) Pass the following Ohio Assessments for Educators (OAE): Special Education Content Exam (#043) Foundations of Reading Exam (#190) While testing may be completed at any time, most candidates choose to take the content exams after completing most or all of their coursework. ​ Apply Advancing Literacy Through the Science of Reading The Reading Endorsement at Lake Erie College prepares licensed educators to become literacy specialists equipped to support struggling readers and strengthen literacy instruction across grade levels. This 18-credit endorsement program integrates classroom-based field experiences and emphasizes the essential components of effective literacy instruction. The program is fully aligned with the Science of Reading, ensuring that educators are prepared to implement evidence-based practices grounded in cognitive science and literacy research. Coursework reflects the standards of the International Literacy Association (ILA), the International Dyslexia Association (IDA), and the guidelines established by the Ohio Department of Higher Education. Candidates learn proven methods for supporting reading development, identifying reading difficulties, and providing effective instruction for students with dyslexia and other literacy challenges. Through coursework focused on phonics, reading assessment, literacy strategies, and socio-cultural influences on reading, educators develop the knowledge and skills necessary to help all students develop strong communication abilities, including reading, writing, listening, speaking, and visual literacy. The Reading Endorsement may be added to any standard Ohio teaching license, including Primary Education (PK–5), Middle Childhood (4–9), Intervention Specialist (K–12), Adolescence to Young Adult (7–12), and Multi-Age (PK–12). ​ Coursework in Reading Instruction Students complete graduate coursework in evidence-based literacy instruction, including: Theory and Practice of Reading Reading Strategies and Curriculum Assessments in Reading Instruction Socio-Cultural Factors in Literacy Phonics Professional Development and Field Research ​ What You Can Do with the Endorsement Educators who complete the Reading Endorsement are prepared to strengthen literacy outcomes for students and serve in roles such as: Reading or Literacy Specialist Intervention Teacher for Struggling Readers Literacy Coach or Reading Support Teacher Curriculum Specialist in Literacy Instruction Educator Supporting Evidence-Based Reading Instruction ​ Apply Teaching English Learners in Today’s Classrooms The TESOL / ESOL Endorsement at Lake Erie College prepares licensed educators to effectively support students whose first language is not English. This 18-credit endorsement program equips teachers with the knowledge, skills, and cultural understanding necessary to meet the needs of English learners in increasingly diverse classrooms. Through coursework in second language acquisition, linguistics, culturally responsive teaching, and assessment of English learners, educators learn how to design instruction that promotes language development and academic success. The program emphasizes practical classroom strategies, legal and policy considerations, and research-based approaches to teaching English learners across grade levels. The endorsement may be added to an existing Ohio teaching license, allowing educators to work more effectively with multilingual students and support schools serving diverse populations. ​ Coursework in TESOL / ESOL Students complete 18 semester hours of coursework, including: Sociocultural Awareness in the Classroom Second Language Acquisition and Development Methods and Materials for Teaching English to Speakers of Other Languages Linguistics Assessment and Evaluation TESOL Practicum and Seminar ​ What You Can Do with the Endorsement Educators who complete the TESOL / ESOL Endorsement are prepared to support English learners in a variety of educational settings, including roles such as: English Learner (EL) / ESOL Teacher TESOL Specialist English Language Development Instructor Classroom Teacher Supporting Multilingual Learners School or District English Learner Support Specialist ​ Apply Bachelor’s Degree in Elementary Education or Mild to Moderate Intervention Specialist Imagine earning your teaching degree while gaining real classroom experience. Lake Erie College’s Bachelor’s in Elementary and Special Education, powered by BloomBoard, allows paraeducators to complete their degree while working in schools as instructional assistants or long-term substitute teachers. Designed for educators with an associate degree, this on-the-job, cohort-based distance learning program blends coursework, coaching, and classroom practice so candidates can learn and earn college credit alongside their students—gradually assuming full teaching responsibilities. ​ Program Benefits Flexibly Paced Complete much of your coursework during the school day with a schedule that fits your professional life. Classroom-Centered Apply what you learn immediately, integrating coursework into your daily classroom practice. Personalized Support Access individualized guidance through faculty mentorship, professional learning communities, and discussion groups. ​ About BloomBoard BloomBoard is a national talent development provider that partners with school districts and colleges to offer on-the-job degree and certification programs. Their innovative model empowers educators to advance their careers through real classroom experience. ​ Get Started Office of Financial Aid Visit Campus Overview Apply Request Info 391 W Washington St, Painesville, Ohio, 44077 440.375.7000 © 2026 Lake Erie College Campus Map Catalog and School Handbook Compliments and Complaints Consumer Information Make a Payment APPLY VISIT Give Lake Erie Online Close About History News and Announcements Faculty & Staff Board of Directors Accreditation Events Admissions Campus Tours Tuition & Fees Housing & Dining Financial Aid College Credit Plus (CCP) Office of Veterans and Military Service New Student Orientation Academics Academic Calendar Undergraduate Degrees Graduate Degrees Physician Assistant Program (PA) Study Abroad Partnerships & Pre-Professional Programs Professional Teacher Pathway Registrar Student Life Clubs & Organizations Tower Honors Program Career Development Career Closet Lincoln Learning Commons Campus Safety Non-Degree Programs Professional Development for Teachers Equine Studies Athletics Arts & Culture Alumni & Friends Parents & Family Careers Facilities Bookstore Campus Gear LAKE ERIE ONLINE bottom of page Double click the content to see meaning"
    }
